1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
Welcome back. A tool's input schema gives you a few things to get right. The fields, their types,

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,000
and which ones are required.

3
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Nail them, and Claude hands you clean arguments. Miss them, and you fight malformed calls over and over.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
This lesson walks through designing a schema that constrains the model.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Let's start with what an input schema is actually for. It does three jobs.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:31,000
First, it declares the fields. The schema lists every parameter your tool accepts, and each one gets

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:34,000
a name that Claude fills in when it calls the tool.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:42,000
Second, it types each field. Every parameter is a string, an integer, a Boolean, an array, or an

9
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,000
object, so the model knows the exact shape to supply.

10
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
Third, it marks which fields are required. The required list names the parameters Claude can't leave

11
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:55,000
out, and everything else is treated as optional.

12
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Declare the fields, type them, and mark what's required. That's the contract Claude fills.

13
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Here's something that trips up developers new to tool use. When Claude decides to call your tool, it

14
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:11,000
doesn't hand you a sentence describing what it wants.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:17,000
It hands you a JSON object, and that object matches the schema you defined.

16
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:23,000
So, in your code, you're parsing structured data, not scraping freeform text for values.

17
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:29,000
Each key in that object maps directly to a field you declared, with the type you declared.

18
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:33,000
That's the whole point of the schema. It makes the model's output predictable enough to feed

19
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,000
straight into your function.

20
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:43,000
This is why typed fields matter so much. The schema is what turns a request into clean, parsable arguments.

21
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:49,000
So, how do you actually build one of these schemas? Three building blocks do most of the work.

22
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:55,000
The first is types. You declare whether each argument is a string, an integer, a Boolean, an array,

23
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
or an object, so it arrives in the shape your code expects.

24
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,000
The second is enums. When a field has a fixed set of valid values, you list them, and the model can

25
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:08,000
only choose from that set.

26
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The third is per-property descriptions. You add a short description to each field, telling Claude

27
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:16,000
what it means and how to fill it.

28
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:24,000
Types, enums, and field descriptions. Those three give you most of the control you need over the arguments.

29
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Now, a subtle mistake I see constantly is over-marking fields as required. Here's the rule. Reserve

30
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,000
the required list for fields that have no safe default, the values your tool genuinely can't run without.

31
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
A user ID for a lookup, sure, that's required. But if a field has a sensible default, or the tool

32
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:46,000
can work without it, leave it optional.

33
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:52,000
Why does this matter? Because when you mark a field required, and Claude doesn't have a clear value

34
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
for it, the model stops and asks the user instead of just proceeding.

35
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:03,000
Over-requiring turns a smooth call into an interruption. Require what's truly essential, make

36
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:07,000
everything else optional, and the model flows through far more often.

37
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Let's talk about one of the highest leverage moves in schema design, the enum. Whenever a field has

38
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
a fixed set of valid values, use an enum instead of an open string.

39
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:25,000
Think about a status field. If you type it as a plain string, Claude might send active, or capital A

40
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:28,000
active, or enabled, or something you never planned for.

41
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,000
And now your code has to handle all of it. Define it as an enum with exactly the values you accept,

42
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:36,000
and the model can only pick from that list.

43
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,000
Categorical inputs like status, priority, or a region code are perfect candidates. Constrain the

44
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:49,000
choice at the schema level, and you head off a whole class of bad arguments before they ever reach your code.

45
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:56,000
Let's pull the design guidance into three field-level habits. First, narrow the type. Always prefer

46
00:03:56,000 --> 00:03:58,000
the tightest type that fits the data.

47
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:04,000
If a value is always a number, make it an integer, not a string, so the model can't hand you

48
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:06,000
something you have to parse and validate.

49
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Second, constrain with enums. Replace free-form categories with an enum so only values you actually

50
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,000
handle can come through.

51
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Third, describe each property. A short per-field description tells Claude what the value means and

52
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:28,000
how to supply it, which cuts down ambiguous or malformed arguments dramatically.

53
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Narrow the type, constrain with enums, describe each field. Three small habits that add up to consistently valid calls.

54
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:44,000
There's a feature worth knowing when you need guarantees, and it's called strict. When you set

55
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
strict to true on a tool definition, Claude's generated arguments are guaranteed to match your schema exactly.

56
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:57,000
Under the hood, it uses grammar-constrained sampling, which means the model literally can't produce

57
00:04:57,000 --> 00:04:59,000
a value that violates the schema.

58
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:06,000
Missing required parameters and type mismatches simply stop happening. And you can combine it with

59
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:12,000
forcing tool use, so you guarantee both that a tool gets called and that its inputs are schema-valid.

60
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
Without strict, a good schema strongly shapes the arguments. With strict, it becomes a hard

61
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:24,000
guarantee. When malformed calls would be expensive to handle, that guarantee is worth reaching for.

62
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:31,000
Let's close with a distinction that saves a lot of debugging. A schema-valid argument isn't the same

63
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:32,000
as a correct one.

64
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:38,000
When strict or a well-built schema tells you the arguments are structurally valid, that means the

65
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
types are right and every required field is present.

66
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:47,000
That's real, and it's useful. But it says nothing about whether the call will actually succeed.

67
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:53,000
Claude can send you a perfectly valid customer ID that simply doesn't exist, or a valid action that

68
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:55,000
isn't allowed in the current state.

69
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:02,000
The tool can still fail at runtime, so validate the shape with your schema, but never assume a valid

70
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:04,000
shape means a successful outcome.

71
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:08,000
Those are two different checks at two different layers.

72
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:15,000
Here's the bottom line. The input schema is your argument contract, and each part has a job. Types

73
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
shape the values. Enums fix the categories. Required guards the essentials.

74
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:28,000
Just remember, a schema-valid call still isn't guaranteed to succeed at runtime.

75
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,000
That covers JSON schemas for tool inputs. I'll see you in the next one.

